{
  "title": "How do I get there?",
  "title_target": "Як пройти? — Yak proyty?",
  "goal": "Ask people in the street for directions, understand the answer, and use the imperative, which in Ukrainian isn't rude at all as long as one polite word comes with it.",
  "bekal": [
    "ask the way with Як пройти / Як доїхати",
    "form the imperative for ти and ви",
    "understand directions people give you",
    "use prepositions of position (біля, навпроти, за, перед)",
    "name public places in the city"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Діалог — Asking the way on Khreshchatyk",
      "intro": "Ani is looking for a museum and asks a passerby. Notice that all the directions are given in the imperative, and none of them sounds rude.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Вибачте|VI-bach-teh], [як|yak] [пройти|proy-TI] [до|do] [музею|moo-ZEH-yoo]?",
          "id": "Excuse me, how do I get to the museum?",
          "literal": "forgive, how — to-walk-through — to — museum (genitive)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Перехожий:",
          "text": "[До|Do] [якого|ya-KO-ho] [музею|moo-ZEH-yoo]?",
          "id": "Which museum?",
          "literal": "to — which (genitive) — museum?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[До|Do] [Національного|Na-tsee-o-NAL'-no-ho] [музею|moo-ZEH-yoo].",
          "id": "The National Museum.",
          "literal": "to — National (genitive) — museum."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Перехожий:",
          "text": "[Ідіть|EE-deet'] [прямо|PRYA-mo] [до|do] [перехрестя|peh-reh-KHRES-tya], [потім|PO-teem] [поверніть|po-ver-NEET'] [ліворуч|lee-VO-rooch].",
          "id": "Go straight to the intersection, then turn left.",
          "literal": "go — straight — up-to — intersection, then — turn — to-the-left."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Ліворуч|Lee-VO-rooch]... [А|A] [потім|PO-teem]?",
          "id": "Left... And then?",
          "literal": "to-the-left... and — then?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Перехожий:",
          "text": "[Потім|PO-teem] [прямо|PRYA-mo] [метрів|MET-reev] [двісті|DVEES-tee]. [Музей|Moo-ZEY] [буде|BOO-deh] [праворуч|pra-VO-rooch].",
          "id": "Then straight on for about two hundred meters. The museum will be on the right.",
          "literal": "then — straight — meters (genitive) — two-hundred. museum — will-be — on-the-right."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Це|Tseh] [далеко|da-LEH-ko]?",
          "id": "Is it far?",
          "literal": "this — far?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Перехожий:",
          "text": "[Хвилин|Khvi-LIN] [десять|DEH-syat'] [пішки|PEESH-ki].",
          "id": "About ten minutes on foot.",
          "literal": "minutes (genitive) — ten — on-foot."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[А|A] [можна|MOZH-na] [на|na] [автобусі|av-TO-boo-see]?",
          "id": "Can I take a bus?",
          "literal": "and — allowed — on — bus?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Перехожий:",
          "text": "[Можна|MOZH-na], [але|A-leh] [пішки|PEESH-ki] [швидше|SHVID-sheh].",
          "id": "You can, but walking is faster.",
          "literal": "allowed, but — on-foot — faster."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Зрозуміла|Zro-zoo-MEE-la]. [Дуже|DOO-zheh] [дякую|DYA-koo-yoo]!",
          "id": "Got it. Thank you very much!",
          "literal": "understood (past f). very — thank-you!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Перехожий:",
          "text": "[Будь|Bood'] [ласка|LAS-ka]. [Гарного|HAR-no-ho] [дня|dnya]!",
          "id": "You're welcome. Have a nice day!",
          "literal": "be — kindness. nice (genitive) — day!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Як пройти for walking, Як доїхати for vehicles.** The same split as *йти* and *їхати* in Unit 13, and Ukrainians notice it. If in doubt, *Як дістатися до…?* (how do I get to…) covers both.",
        "**The imperative isn't rude in Ukrainian.** *Ідіть прямо* (go straight) is the normal way to give directions, and no one hears it as bossy. What changes the tone is **будь ласка**, and even that is often dropped in directions because it isn't needed.",
        "**Commands to ти and ви differ by one syllable.** *Іди / Ідіть*, *поверни / поверніть*, *скажи / скажіть*, *почекай / почекайте*. The *ви* form adds **-те** to the *ти* form. Since you'll be asking strangers, the **-те** form is the one to master.",
        "**Ліворуч and праворуч, not наліво and направо.** The second pair is Russian and often heard in Ukraine, but the standard Ukrainian forms are **ліворуч** (left) and **праворуч** (right). Both come from *ліва рука* and *права рука*: left hand and right hand.",
        "**Метрів двісті means \"about two hundred meters\".** Flipping the order of number and unit is the Ukrainian way to say \"roughly\": *хвилин десять* (about ten minutes), *днів п'ять* (about five days). Neat, simple, and used all the time.",
        "**Зрозуміла, not зрозуміло.** Ani is a woman, so she uses the feminine past form: \"I've understood\". The neuter *зрозуміло* means \"it's clear\" in general, with no subject. Both are correct here, and the difference is subtle.",
        "**Гарного дня! is a warm goodbye.** It's in родовий (genitive) because it's a fragment of a wish, just like *Доброго дня* in Unit 1. Its relatives: *Гарного вечора*, *Гарних вихідних* (have a nice weekend), *Успіхів* (good luck)."
      ],
